const body = `<h1>6.2 — Ground Checks: No Infinite Jumps</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your jump is limited, and the limit has a hole in it. The budget — <code>jumpsRemaining</code>,
    <code>maxJumps</code>, one refill per landing — is right. What is wrong is <em>how it learns
    you are on the ground</em>: a collision callback that fires when something tagged
    <code>Ground</code> touches you. That callback never tells you when you <strong>leave</strong>,
    so stepping off a ledge leaves you a full jump you did not earn. And it only answers for
    surfaces somebody remembered to tag. Today you replace the ear with a question: a ray, cast
    every frame from the player's feet, filtered to one layer — and then use it to charge one jump
    for walking off.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to check if the player is on the ground, how to raycast downward with physics2d.raycast, how to use a layermask to find ground, how to stop infinite jumping', 2)}

${renderDecompose('6.2', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

