    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Asking Versus Being Told</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which spot in your cave is a poor stand-in for your whole body under one exact point — and what does the player experience there?</li>
            <li>Why is "a full budget while airborne" the same fact as "I walked off"? And what would the rule have to remember if you wrote it the other way?</li>
            <li><code>maxJumps</code> is <code>1</code> today. If you changed it to <code>2</code> right now, would you get a double jump? Name every line that would have to be true for the second jump to actually work — including what the walk-off rule does to a player carrying two.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        There are three common ways to answer <em>"am I standing on something?"</em>. Your file
        uses the first row right now, and the two failures you found in the editor are the two
        holes that row has. Today you move to the third. Read all three — the difference is in
        <em>when</em> each one knows, and what it can tell you when it does.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Technique</th><th>When it knows</th><th>What it can tell you</th><th>Where it lies to you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnCollisionEnter2D()</code> plus <code>CompareTag()</code></td>
            <td>On the frame contact begins — a message from the engine.</td>
            <td>That you touched <em>something</em>, and what that something is named.</td>
            <td>Nothing fires when you <em>leave</em>, so the budget stays full until you land again; and every new surface needs the tag by hand.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>OnTriggerEnter2D()</code> on a small child under your feet</td>
            <td>On the frame the child's trigger overlaps something.</td>
            <td>That a particular point on your body is touching something.</td>
            <td>The child has to be placed by hand, and a trigger that starts inside a collider reports it forever.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Physics2D.Raycast()</code> every frame</td>
            <td>Whenever you ask — every frame, for as long as you want.</td>
            <td>Whether something is under one exact point, <em>how far</em> away it is, and what it is.</td>
            <td>An infinitely thin line: a pixel of your foot over a ledge reports air. It also needs a <code>LayerMask</code> to filter what it may notice.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        The third row is what you build today, and it is what makes the walk-off rule possible:
        a callback only fires when something changes, and "did I leave the ground this frame?"
        is a question you can only ask.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Prove It, Then Break It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — the five jumps.</strong> Press Play and count in each situation: on the
        floor (<em>one, then nothing</em>), landing again (<em>one again</em>), walking off a ledge
        and pressing J (<em>nothing — the walk-off rule charged you</em>), landing on the cave mouth
        (<em>one — the layer caught it</em>), and in mid-air over nothing after a normal jump
        (<em>nothing</em>).
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — watch the number.</strong> Keep the <code>print()</code> in
        <code>Start()</code> and add one to the charge for a minute. Press Play, then walk off a
        ledge: the Console should read the budget drop by one the frame the red line stops hitting.
        Take both prints out before you commit.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the empty mask.</strong> Set <strong>Ground Mask</strong> to
        <code>Nothing</code> and play. The red line still draws, the floor is still there, and you
        can jump forever — nothing errors. Put it back, and say which line of your code was right
        the whole time.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — the charge that is a literal.</strong> Change
        <code>jumpsRemaining -= 1;</code> in the walk-off branch to
        <code>jumpsRemaining = 0;</code>, then later set <code>maxJumps = 2</code>. Walking off now
        costs both jumps. One of those two versions is a game rule and the other is a coincidence
        of today's number.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the budget.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Where does the number of allowed jumps live, and how many places read it? A limit written into a condition is a limit nobody can tune.</li>
            <li>Is the spend inside the same condition as the jump? Say what the player experiences when it is not.</li>
            <li>Which surfaces count as ground, and how does the code know? Name the mechanism — a tag, a layer, a component — and one surface it gets wrong.</li>
            <li>Which of the two budget rules is the refill, and which is the charge? Say what a player loses if you ship only one of them.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Explain why a jump needs a budget rather than a rule</li>
        <li>Say why <code>maxJumps</code> exists as a field and why the refill reads it instead of a literal</li>
        <li>Explain why the decrement sits inside the <code>if</code></li>
        <li>Say what a raycast tells you that a collision callback cannot, and what a <code>LayerMask</code> is for</li>
        <li>Explain why a full budget in mid-air is proof that the player walked off, with no previous frame remembered</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

